# Proof that freeing a large object releases its mapping — the second half of
# the large-object check in GC_DESIGN.md's Stage 2 verification, split into
# its own program because the observation is the process's death.
#
#   ./bin/iyi build -Dgc_iyi bench/arena_large_probe.iyi
#
# The program allocates over the top size class, frees through
# `IyiHeap.free`, and then reads the first byte of the mapping. If `munmap`
# released it, that read faults and the kernel kills the process with
# SIGSEGV, which `bench/arena_exercise.sh` asserts. If the read prints, the
# mapping survived and free is broken. Nothing allocates between the free
# and the read, so nothing can remap the region first and make a released
# mapping look held. Built only with `-Dgc_iyi`: the default allocator has
# no free for this to probe.

@[Unsafe("reads the first byte of a freed large Pointer.malloc block to prove munmap released its mapping")]
big = 2097153
large = Pointer(UInt8).malloc(big.to_u64)
large[0] = 7_u8
large[big - 1] = 9_u8
IyiHeap.free(large.as(Void*))
byte = large[0]
puts "iyi: a freed large mapping survived the read (got #{byte.to_i32}); munmap did not release it"
